# TASK-260715-ypo7yo — handoff evidence (round 2)

Role: `solution-architect` (`analyst`). Produced 2026-07-28 on macOS 26.5 (25F71),
Xcode 26.5 (17F42). **Ready for review** — this is rework against
`TASK-260715-ypo7yo_reviewer-verdict-01.md` (CHANGES REQUESTED), not a fresh
submission. An independent architecture reviewer must accept the matrix before any
portal mutation.

## Verdict 01 rework — all five required items

| # | required rework | done |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Model NE values per signing/profile channel, incl. exact C1 development and later Developer ID rows | §4.0–4.1 + `signingChannels`, `networkExtensionRule`, per-target `valueByChannel`, per-profile `authorizesNetworkExtensionValues` |
| 2 | Make the App Group model consistent with current Apple semantics; prefer registered bare `group.` on both platforms | §4.3 + `appGroupStyleRule`; both literals are now `group.works.relux.tunnel` / `.probe` |
| 3 | Replace A10 with explicit per-target/channel authored and generated allowlists | §9.1 + `entitlementClassification`; A10 → A10a/A10b/A10c/A10d |
| 4 | Correct the App Sandbox rationale | §4.2 D-4 + `requirementSource` / `requirementBasis` on every row |
| 5 | Extend validator and negative gates so each defect fails closed; update handoff evidence; preserve accepted decisions | R7/R19/R22/R23, R9/R10/R18, R20, R21; 23 negative gates; §13 |

### F1 — Network Extension entitlement by signing channel

**Confirmed, then fixed.** Apple DTS states the value depends on distribution
channel: `-systemextension` for direct Developer ID signing, unsuffixed
"including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for
development" (<https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/800887>, quoted verbatim
in §4.1). Every NE value is now keyed by channel. The four macOS C1 rows carry
`packet-tunnel-provider`; the Developer ID rows carry
`packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension`. C1's authorized values are pinned to the
development-channel set, so the ceremony can no longer be pointed at the wrong
value.

Independently corroborated on this Mac: Tailscale, PureVPN and Surfshark — three
shipping Developer-ID-distributed packet-tunnel products — all carry the suffixed
value on **both** host and embedded provider.

### F2 — App Group identifier style

**Confirmed, then fixed.** Apple DTS: iOS-style IDs begin `group.` and are
allocated on the Developer website; macOS-style IDs begin with the Team ID and
"can't be explicitly allocated on the Developer website"; since 21 Feb 2025
iOS-style IDs are fully supported on macOS and are the Xcode 16.3+ default
(<https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/721701>, quoted verbatim in §4.3).
The macOS literal is now the registered record itself, so the "same record,
different literal" conflation is gone. R18 now asserts literal == portal record on
both platforms.

**One caveat recorded rather than suppressed:** every Apple example of an App
Group used as a sandboxed **Mach service name prefix**, and all three shipping
products here, use the macOS-style Team-ID form — all predating Feb 2025. No
source consulted confirms an iOS-style ID works for that purpose. This project has
no XPC Mach service today (`.spec/architecture.md:111` — the host↔provider channel
is an App Group snapshot plus Keychain references), so the decision stands, but it
is logged as **OC-4** with owner `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` and a concrete amendment
path (a separate, unregistered macOS-style ID for that single purpose, no portal
record, no C1 dependency).

### F3 — A10 was not executable

**Confirmed, then fixed.** A10 is replaced by a computed allowlist (§9.1) plus
four assertions: completeness, containment, exclusion, and channel hygiene.
Signing-generated keys established empirically on this Mac —
`com.apple.application-identifier` and `com.apple.developer.team-identifier` on
every signed macOS bundle inspected, `com.apple.security.get-task-allow` on the
development channel only. iOS spellings are recorded at **medium confidence** with
`TASK-260715-3dno4w` named as verifier, because no iOS bundle was built here.

### F4 — App Sandbox rationale

**Confirmed, and the evidence is stronger than the finding.** All three shipping
Developer ID hosts on this Mac carry `com.apple.security.app-sandbox = false`;
all three providers carry `true`. Provider sandboxing is now attributed to
`apple-requirement`; host sandboxing to `project-architecture`, citing
`.spec/platform-distribution.md` and ADR-018. Network client/server rows are
`sandbox-consequence`. Validator R21 rejects a host row claiming an Apple
requirement. This also sharpened OC-2: no inspected product exercises the
sandboxed-host + `system-extension.install` combination.

