.spec/architecture.md-106-## Control and state ownership
.spec/architecture.md-107-
.spec/architecture.md-108-The extension is the authority for live state. The containing app persists
.spec/architecture.md-109-profiles and requests actions but derives connection status from
.spec/architecture.md-110-`NETunnelProviderSession` plus a versioned app-message snapshot. Shared mutable
.spec/architecture.md:111:state is limited to an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references.
.spec/architecture.md-112-
.spec/architecture.md-113-## Platform-intent gate
.spec/architecture.md-114-
.spec/architecture.md:115:Apple documents packet tunnel providers as packet-oriented custom VPNs whose
.spec/architecture.md-116-packets are sent to a tunnel server. Apple Technical Note TN3120 warns against
.spec/architecture.md-117-claiming packet traffic and proxying it through another interface. Relux's
.spec/architecture.md-118-local TCP termination followed by SSH `direct-tcpip` may be interpreted as that
.spec/architecture.md-119-discouraged model even though SSH is the custom tunnel and the exit is remote.
.spec/architecture.md-120-
.spec/architecture.md-121-**Gate A0:** obtain evidence that this architecture is acceptable for the
.spec/architecture.md:122:intended Network Extension entitlement and App Store distribution. Evidence may
.spec/architecture.md-123-be a written Apple Developer Technical Support response, successful
.spec/architecture.md:124:entitlement/review feedback for an accurately disclosed prototype, or an
.spec/architecture.md-125-architecture change that sends packet semantics to a remote VPN endpoint. This
.spec/architecture.md-126-is a release viability gate, not a hidden implementation assumption.
.spec/architecture.md-127-
.spec/architecture.md-128-**Scope of Gate A0 (re-scoped 2026-07-28 by ADR-013/ADR-024).** A0 is an App
.spec/architecture.md-129-Store / App Review release gate. It is **deferred** for the macOS prototype goal
